What happens inside the Transformer?

The Transformer processes token representations through multiple layers. Three important ideas are involved.


Multiple layer example: 
Input token representations
The · customer · needs · a · home · loan
|
Transformer Layer 1
Processes token information using attention and neural-network operations.
|
Transformer Layer 2
Transforms the representations further using contextual information.
|
Transformer Layer 3 ... Layer N
Repeated processing builds richer contextual representations.
|
Final representations → output prediction
The model calculates scores for possible next tokens.


A. Embeddings
Convert token IDs into vectors (lists of numbers) that the model can process. Positional information helps the model represent the order of tokens.

B. Self-attention
Helps each token representation use information from relevant earlier tokens. For example, in "Priya deposited money because she received her salary," context helps relate "she" to Priya.

Consider these two sentences:
- "The customer deposited money into the bank."
- "The customer sat on the bank of the river."
The word bank has different meanings in each sentence. The surrounding words help the model represent the correct context.
Attention helps the model use those relationships

C. Feed-forward networks
Further transform the representations at each position, helping the model learn complex patterns.
