Retrieval can still return irrelevant information, and the LLM can still make mistakes. Production systems need source validation, access controls, evaluation, and appropriate fallback behavior.

Q1. How does an LLM generate text?
An autoregressive LLM tokenizes the input, processes token representations through Transformer layers, calculates scores for the next token, and selects a token using a decoding strategy. It repeats this process until a stopping condition is reached.
Q2. What is the difference between logits and probabilities?
Logits are raw scores produced for candidate tokens. Softmax can transform logits into a probability distribution over the vocabulary.
Q3. What is the role of temperature?
Temperature adjusts the probability distribution used during decoding. Lower values generally favor high-probability tokens, while higher values generally allow more varied selections.
Q4. Why does an LLM need attention?
Attention helps the model use contextual relationships between tokens to construct useful representations of the input.
Q5. Why do we need RAG if an LLM can generate answers?
An LLM may not know private, updated, or domain-specific information. RAG retrieves relevant external information and supplies it to the model as context, improving its ability to answer questions based on those sources.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Prompt → Tokens → Embeddings → Transformer and attention → Logits → Probabilities → Token selection → Repeat → Response
